+          <div class="section-content">
+            <div class="task-group">
+              <ul class="item-container">
+                <li class="item">
+                  <div>
+                    <code>
+                    <a name="/s:10Foundation12CharacterSetV9AlamofireE17afURLQueryAllowedACvpZ"></a>
+                    <a name="//apple_ref/swift/Variable/afURLQueryAllowed" class="dashAnchor"></a>
+                    <a class="token" href="#/s:10Foundation12CharacterSetV9AlamofireE17afURLQueryAllowedACvpZ">afURLQueryAllowed</a>
+                    </code>
+                  </div>
+                  <div class="height-container">
+                    <div class="pointer-container"></div>
+                    <section class="section">
+                      <div class="pointer"></div>
+                      <div class="abstract">
+                        <p>Creates a CharacterSet from RFC 3986 allowed characters.</p>
+
+<p>RFC 3986 states that the following characters are <q>reserved</q> characters.</p>
+
+<ul>
+<li>General Delimiters: <q>:</q>, <q>#</q>, <q>[</q>, <q>]</q>, <q>@</q>, <q>?</q>, <q>/</q></li>
+<li>Sub-Delimiters: <q>!</q>, <q>$</q>, <q>&</q>, <q>&lsquo;</q>, <q>(</q>, <q>)</q>, <q>*</q>, <q>+</q>, <q>,</q>, <q>;</q>, <q>=</q></li>
+</ul>
+
+<p>In RFC 3986 - Section 3.4, it states that the <q>?</q> and <q>/</q> characters should not be escaped to allow
+query strings to include a URL. Therefore, all <q>reserved</q> characters with the exception of <q>?</q> and <q>/</q>
+should be percent-escaped in the query string.</p>
+
+                      </div>
